diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any loss of reachability from the access VLAN to the data center. Host host_1_1_1_1 is fully connected upstream and to the DC segment. From the host, ICMP to the DC DNS server (10.200.0.2) succeeds with 0% loss and ~1.48 ms RTT, and HTTP to web_server_0 (10.200.0.3) completes in 3–6 ms total. Topology-wide probes also show host_1_1_1_1 reaching DC servers (e.g., web_server_3) without loss.

The suspected distribution uplink (switch_dist_1_1:eth0 to router_core_1:eth2) is up. Evidence:
- OSPF on switch_dist_1_1 shows a Full adjacency with router_core_1 over eth0 (neighbor 172.16.0.0 at 172.16.0.7), and OSPF routes to 10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 are installed.
- OSPF on router_core_1 is Full with switch_dist_1_1 on eth2 and has a route to 10.1.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.7.
- ethtool reports Link detected: yes at 10Gb/s on both ends (switch_dist_1_1:eth0 and router_core_1:eth2).

Mechanistically, if that uplink were down, OSPF would drop to Down and routes to 10.200.0.0/24 would vanish, and pings/HTTP from host_1_1_1_1 would fail—none of which is observed. Localization: no faulty device or interface found; the core, distribution, and server access paths are operational.

Root cause: No active fault present; any outage was transient or outside this path.

Corrective fix: None required now. No device or link needs restarting. If the symptom recurs, monitor and check for interface flaps specifically on switch_dist_1_1:eth0 and router_core_1:eth2. 
=== END ===